  "## Purpose

A decision brief for the three collisions ADR-2608120800 records. It does not
decide them; it makes deciding cheap. **The main finding is that they are three
different problems with three different answers, and resolving them together
would be wrong.**

## The framing in the earlier ADR needs one correction

**These are not extractions from `kami-engine-sdk`.** I assumed they were. Git
says otherwise, and it changes what the collision means.

- `webgpu` grew `kami.fsm` on 2026-06-22 and `kami.gpu` on 2026-06-25.
- On 2026-07-10 both were deleted from `webgpu` and added to the new repos,
  commit message `kami.* SSoT extract from webgpu`. `host` got `kami.input` the
  same day.
- The SDK's three files arrived in ONE commit on 2026-07-02: *migrate
  kami-engine-sdk-clj out of the kami-engine monorepo*.

**Two independent lineages** — the `webgpu` family and the `kami-engine`
monorepo — that collided on the `kami.*` prefix. ADR-2607102200's extraction
table lists only `webgpu` packages; the SDK appears nowhere in it. So the SDK's
copy was **neither left behind deliberately nor by omission** — it was never in
scope. **Nobody has ever decided that these two sets should coexist**, which is
why no README mentions the other side.

## There is a fifth tree, and it is the one that runs

Checkout, west pin and the child's `main` are the usual three. There is a
fourth: the `:git/sha` each consumer pins in its own `deps.edn`, materialised in
`~/.gitlibs`. Those are older than all of the above and **no amount of syncing
fixes them**. For `host` the pinned gitlib ships `kami/input.cljs` where `main`
ships `.cljc` — which matters, because ClojureScript prefers `.cljs` over
`.cljc` REGARDLESS of classpath order.

## The winner is not fixed — it flips by consumer

Measured with real `clojure -Spath` in `webgpu`: **extracted wins**. In isekai's
dependency shape: **SDK wins**, because isekai declares the SDK as a direct
top-level `:local/root` (under both coordinate names), which sorts ahead of
`webgpu`'s transitive gitlibs.

So `the SDK wins the classpath` is true in isekai and false in webgpu. **Neither
winner is what its consumer expects** — `webgpu`'s test wants the extracted API
and gets it by luck of ordering.

## The three, and why they are not one problem

### `kami.fsm` — same name, same arity, opposite meaning

Only `advance` is shared, and both have a 3-arity. The extracted one takes a
SET of events and returns a keyword; the SDK's takes a SINGLE event and returns
a map. **That is the dangerous shape**: no error at the call site. A set where
the SDK wants one event matches no row and silently returns the state unchanged;
one keyword where the extracted lib wants a set makes `contains?` throw.

The isekai claim is verified exactly: `kotoba.fsm` re-exports four vars, and if
the SDK wins, **three do not exist** — undefined vars, and in ClojureScript a
`TypeError` at boot. The fourth resolves to the wrong function.

Consumers: the extracted API has one, a `webgpu` test. The SDK API has one, the
SDK's own contract test. **The `kotoba.fsm` facade has zero consumers
fleet-wide.**

### `kami.gpu` — zero shared names, and no clean answer

Nothing overlaps, so the loser fails loudly rather than silently. But they are
different KINDS: the extracted one is a capability/render-graph data contract
(11 defs); the SDK's is a runtime protocol (`IGpuBackend`, 7 methods).

**Deleting the SDK's copy breaks `kotoba-lang/host`** — which is itself the SSoT
of `kami.input` and a dependency of both `webgpu` and isekai. The dependency
runs `webgpu -> host -> SDK`. Both sides are live and the names do not overlap,
so neither can absorb the other without a rename. **This is the only one of the
three that genuinely needs the owner rather than a cleanup.**

Worth noting: `webgl` requires `kotoba.gpu` — a THIRD copy that owns its
implementation rather than re-exporting, so it survives either resolution. A
pre-paid hedge against exactly this decision.

### `kami.input` — the cheap one

Zero shared names. The `host` version has four live consumers across two apps.
**The SDK version has one consumer anywhere: the SDK's own contract test.**
Deleting the SDK's copy is the cheapest of all six options and the only one with
zero fleet-wide consequences.

## The workaround, and a cost the earlier ADR does not record

`network-isekai/src/isekai/player_fsm.cljc` exists only because of this
collision, and it is live — required by `game.cljc`, used at four call sites,
covered by its own test. It reimplements the extracted lib's exact 4-var API.

It is removable **if and only if** `kami.fsm` resolves to the extracted lib in
isekai's build. Today it resolves to the SDK.

**But pointing isekai at the extracted lib is no longer a pure deletion.** The
extracted `advance` now delegates to a compiled Kotoba profile through
`kami.fsm-oracle`, and its own docstring states that on ClojureScript **the host
must call `register-kir!` first or `advance` throws**. That requirement landed
2026-08-12 and **did not exist on 2026-07-10 when the workaround was written**.
So the fix needs that registration wired into isekai's boot, or it trades an
undefined-var crash for a throw. **This is the single most decision-relevant
fact that was not previously recorded.**

**A fourth copy exists and is dead**: `isekai/game.cljc` lines 90-98 define four
private FSM vars that nothing calls — leftover from an earlier round of the same
workaround. Removable today, unconditionally, under any resolution.

## A blind spot in the detector

`kami/backend/browser` is the same hazard, live in isekai, and **the detector
cannot see it**: `host` ships `.cljc`, the SDK ships `.cljs`. The detector keys
on the relative path INCLUDING EXTENSION, so these are two keys rather than one
collision. But ClojureScript prefers `.cljs` regardless of order — precisely the
failure isekai's own `deps.edn` documents having been bitten by. The same gap
hid `kami/input` for `webgpu`. **The key should be the namespace, not the
filename.**

## Shape of the cheapest resolution (not a decision)

1. **`kami.input`** — delete the SDK's copy. Cost: one SDK contract test.
2. **`kami.fsm`** — delete the SDK's copy AND budget the `register-kir!` wiring
   in isekai, then remove `player_fsm.cljc` and the dead block. Without the
   second half the workaround stays and the fix has not finished.
3. **`kami.gpu`** — do not resolve by deletion. Needs a rename, and an owner.

Deleting the EXTRACTED libraries is worse in all three cases: it discards the
`.kotoba`/KIR work, kills `kami.gpu.launch`, breaks two `webgpu` tests, and
makes isekai's workaround permanent.

## What could not be determined

- **isekai's real classpath.** `clojure -Spath` fails there: a `:local/root` for
  `org-threejs` is not checked out. The isekai orderings above are from a
  reproduced dependency shape, high-confidence but **not measured on the real
  graph**. The `webgpu` orderings are real.
- **`ADR-0040` / `clj-wgsl-ledger`**, which the SDK's docstrings cite to justify
  themselves, resolves to nothing — no such file, no name match in 4,100 repos.
  ADR-2608120800's open question about a future CLJ-to-WASM path **rests on a
  citation that cannot be resolved.**
- No build was run; breakage is from the require graph and var existence, not
  execution.

## Search note for whoever repeats this

**`kami.fsm` cannot be found by substring across 4,100 repos.** `fsm`, `gpu`,
`input`, `advance` are generic English words; fleet-wide greps returned dozens
of unrelated repos. The reliable query is the **require form** —
`grep -rln \"\\[kami\\.fsm\"` — then read alias occurrences within only those
files. That cut the candidate set from ~30 repos to 6 files. Docstring mentions
survive even that filter and must be excluded by eye."}]
